  [1mVerification:[0m [38;5;153mnode format.test.js[39m — all assertions pass. I watched the 6 behavioral assertions fail first (the 2
  "leaves it alone" cases passed at RED, correctly, since they assert no-op behavior). Nothing is committed.

  [1mWhat it does:[0m

  [38;5;153mformat("The quick brown fox", { truncate: 15 })  → "The quick..."[39m
  [38;5;153mformat("hello world",         { truncate: 9 })   → "hello..."        (no space before the ellipsis)[39m
  [38;5;153mformat("supercalifragilistic",{ truncate: 10 })  → "superca..."      (hard-cut fallback)[39m
  [38;5;153mformat("hello",               { truncate: 3 })   → "hel"             (no room to mark the cut)[39m
  [38;5;153mformat("world", { prefix: "hello ", truncate: 8 }) → "hello..."      (measures the final string)[39m

  Two notes on the implementation:

  - The guard is [38;5;153mtypeof options.truncate === "number"[39m, not a truthiness check like the other options. [38;5;153mtruncate: 0[39m is a
    meaningful value and a truthy guard would silently ignore it.
  - Truncation runs after [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m, which is forced by your "never exceeds maxLength" choice. A long prefix
    therefore eats the budget and can cut the suffix off entirely.

  [1mNot changed, but worth knowing:[0m [38;5;153mnode format.test.js[39m prints a [38;5;153mMODULE_TYPELESS_PACKAGE_JSON[39m warning and exits 0 even
  when assertions fail — both predate this change. The obvious fix for the warning ([38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m)
  would break [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, which are CommonJS, so I left it alone. The exit-code issue means CI would
  not catch a regression here; say the word if you want the test file switched to something that fails loudly.

